From 4aa6ac3700c7a880bd022f810a98da35f185dcec Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DrShadow34 <26345330+DrShadow34@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 23:23:08 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] panvk: v9 render-pass overlap (vertex/tiler and fragment
 chains in parallel)

Every batch's vertex/tiler/compute submit waited for the queue's last
submit, the previous batch's fragment chain, and the kernel added an
implicit write dependency on every BO of a submit (device-wide ones like
the sample positions are in all of them): JS1 and JS0 never ran at the
same time. A frame's GPU time was the sum of its vertex/tiler and fragment
work instead of about the larger of the two.

On kernels with linux-cezanne's PANFROST_JD_REQ_SHARED_IMPLICIT_SYNC
(patch 0081, DRM_PANFROST_PARAM_SHARED_IMPLICIT_SYNC; PANVK_JM_OVERLAP=0
turns it off): the kernel only takes implicit
dependencies on dma-buf shared BOs and the queue orders its own chains:
- each slot runs its chains in order; a vertex/tiler/compute chain waits
  for the previous one, a fragment chain for the last vertex/tiler/compute
  chain and the previous fragment chain;
- two device tiler heaps (the second only with overlap), alternating per
  batch that tiles (polygon lists and the IDVS varyings live there): a
  batch tiles while the previous one's fragment job still reads the other
  heap, and waits for the last fragment chain that read its own;
- a vertex/tiler/compute chain waits for all earlier fragment chains when
  a query command (reset, end, copy) or a timestamp whose stage may run in
  fragment jobs asks for it, or after a pipeline barrier whose source may
  run in fragment jobs and whose destination in vertex/tiler/compute jobs
  (transfers count on both sides: vk_meta blits, clears, resolves and MSAA
  copies are fragment jobs) - but then only if it can see what those jobs
  touched: it has compute jobs (dispatches, copies, helper kernels), a
  vertex shader accessing memory (textures, images, storage / texel
  buffers, global: vs.reads_memory, scanned at compile time and kept in
  the pipeline cache), or earlier fragment shaders wrote memory (vertex /
  index / indirect buffers). A skipped barrier stays pending for the
  chains after it. DXVK's broad barriers (all graphics + compute +
  transfer stages) between render passes no longer serialise them;
- queue->sync (fences, semaphores, vkQueueWaitIdle) and event signals are
  the merge of the last chain of each kind (sync_file merge).

Not covered: memory aliasing between an image a fragment job wrote and a
vertex / index buffer read after a barrier (only fragment shader stores
make those wait).

v9 only: Bifrost's indirect draws keep varyings in one device buffer the
fragment jobs read. PANVK_JM_OVERLAP=0 and older kernels: the previous
fully serial submission. PANVK_DEBUG=startup logs the mode.

Tests (tests/vkoverlap: slow fragment pass, then the dependent access on
the vertex/tiler/compute side: vertex shader texture read, compute read,
copy, occlusion query copy, write after read, a barrier skipped by a pass
without memory access and the dispatch after it, a fragment shader store
read as a vertex buffer): no failures with or without overlap, one submit
per frame or one for all; each insufficient-barrier control (BAD, BAD2,
BAD3) reads stale data in 40/40 frames with overlap and 0 without.
Synthetic heavy vertex + heavy fragment passes 59 -> 49 ms per frame, also
with DXVK-style broad barriers.
VK-GL-CTS 1.4.6.2 with overlap: the ordering groups in full (654k cases:
synchronization, synchronization2, memory, query_pool, renderpasses,
draw, compute, multiview, copy_and_blit, image_clearing, ...) and a 1/5
sample of vk-default: every failure also fails without overlap. Dark Souls
Remastered (DXVK): 27.9 -> 26.2 ms per frame.
